const postContent = "<p><strong>Tool scope reviewed 18 November 2026:</strong> The linked Salary Offer Evaluator still uses historical TY 2026 / FY 2025–26 salary rules, for income earned 1 July 2025–30 June 2026. It has not been migrated to current FY 2026–27 payroll. Use the <a href=\"/tools/tax-calculator\">main year-specific salary-tax calculator</a> for its supported periods and ask payroll to confirm your actual treatment. City budgets are illustrative; no live cost feed is connected.</p>\n<h2>Negotiate the Weak Point, Not Everything at Once</h2>\n<p>Many people know an offer feels close but still negotiate vaguely. That makes the conversation weaker than it needs to be. A better approach is to identify <strong>which variable actually changes the decision</strong>.</p>\n<h2>The Usual Negotiation Levers</h2>\n<ul><li>fixed monthly salary</li><li>joining bonus or relocation support</li><li>remote days or commute flexibility</li><li>medical cover</li><li>fuel or transport support</li><li>learning budget or professional development</li></ul>\n<h2>When to Ask for Salary vs Flexibility</h2>\n<p>If tax and city-cost pressure are the main issue, a stronger fixed salary or recurring allowance matters most. If the offer is being weakened by commute burden and energy loss, remote flexibility may improve the decision faster than a small salary adjustment.</p>\n<h2>What Makes Negotiation More Credible</h2>\n<p>Do not negotiate with only emotion. Show that you evaluated the package on after-tax value, benefits, commute, and practical quality of life. That makes the request sound reasoned instead of impulsive.</p>\n<h2>A Good Framing</h2>\n<p>You are not asking for “more because why not”. You are trying to close the specific gap that currently makes the role hard to justify.</p>\n<h2>Use the Evaluator First</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/tools/salary-offer-calculator-pakistan\">Salary Offer Evaluator Pakistan</a> helps you see whether base salary, commute support, or remote flexibility is the variable that improves the decision most. That gives you a much better negotiation brief.</p>\n<p>See which part of the offer actually needs to change before you negotiate.</p>\n<p>Open the evaluator →</p>\n<h2>Separate Cash, Benefits And One-Time Support</h2>\n<p>Compare recurring after-tax cash against a household budget you have checked yourself. Keep insurance cover, provident fund and one-time relocation support in separate columns. They may have value, but cannot all be spent each month. The evaluator's package-minus-example-budget result is not a savings forecast, and its weighted scores are not probabilities. Avoid counting an allowance twice if it is already included in gross salary.</p>\n<p>FBR's <a href=\"https://www.fbr.gov.pk/withholding-tax-rate-card/174298/174301\">official withholding rate-card index</a>, checked 18 November 2026, lists TY 2027 as current and TY 2026 among previous years. The detailed circular and current rate-card PDFs could not be retrieved during this review. No new tax rates or eligibility claims are asserted here. Current-year migration remains a separate source-verification task.</p>"

